(i) A chasmogamous flower has exposed anthers and stigma, so in a bisexual chasmogamous flower all three kinds of pollination can occur:
- **Autogamy**: transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of the **same flower**.
- **Geitonogamy**: transfer of pollen grains from the anther of one flower to the stigma of **another flower of the same plant**. It is functionally cross-pollination involving a pollinating agent, but genetically it is similar to autogamy, since the pollen comes from the same plant.
- **Xenogamy**: transfer of pollen grains from the anther of a flower to the stigma of a flower on a **different plant of the same species**. It brings genetically different pollen to the stigma.

(ii) A mature pollen grain shed at the two-celled stage is drawn as a rounded grain with a two-layered wall and two cells inside. The four labels:
- **exine**, the hard outer layer made of sporopollenin, interrupted at the **germ pores**;
- **intine**, the thin, continuous inner layer of cellulose and pectin;
- **vegetative cell**, the large cell with abundant food reserve and a large, irregularly shaped nucleus;
- **generative cell**, the small, spindle-shaped cell with dense cytoplasm and a nucleus, floating in the cytoplasm of the vegetative cell.
